body:
I wonder if there is a serialization issue that is causing the literals to be slightly different than 3?

For context, mod is passed directly on to GLSL, we don't touch it at all. So this code should be reflected directly in the GLSL.

It could also be a driver issue. AMD drivers on Windows are really bad for Vulkan/OpenGL

Tested the MRP on an M2 Macbook and it works correctly (it is green whether `bugged_mod` is true or false)

Tested on a Windows 10 machine with an AMD RX 6900 XT and I _could_ reproduce the issue. 

Tested on a Linux machine with Intel XE Graphics and I could not reproduce the issue.

Further here is the generated GLSL code:

```
	{
		float m_a=3.0;
		float m_b=3.0;
		float m_mod_result=(material.m_bugged_mod?mod(m_a, m_b):mod(3.0, 3.0));
		if ((m_mod_result == 3.0))
		{
					{
			color.r=1.0;
		}
;
		}
		if ((m_mod_result == 0.0))
		{
					{
			color.g=1.0;
		}
;
		}
	}
```

There are clearly some formatting errors, but it's clear that Godot hasn't done anything to the code that would create this issue. This is GLSL code that gets sent directly to the GPU driver's shader compiler (when using the compatibility renderer). That means this is a driver bug, potentially exclusive to AMD GPUs (I don't have an NVidia device that I can easily test on, so I can't check if it reproduces with NVidia)
